# Line endings, for the Windows path (docs/GPU-RUNBOOK.md §0-W).
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, so a clone made there rewrites
# every checked-out text file to CRLF. Everything in this repository is then run
# under Linux — in WSL, or in a container — and a shell script with CRLF endings
# fails as `bash: \r: command not found`, which names neither the file nor the
# real cause. The same carriage returns land inside SQL migrations, Dockerfiles
# and config.pbtxt, where they fail more quietly still.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository AND in the working tree
# on every platform, whatever the local autocrlf setting says. Binary files are
# detected and left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files where a stray \r is fatal rather than untidy.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.pbtxt   text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
Makefile  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# The one exception to eol=lf, and it is not cosmetic. cmd.exe reads a batch
# file as it executes it, seeking by byte offset — with LF-only endings a
# `goto`, an `if (...)` block or a label can land mid-line and the script runs
# the wrong branch or dies with a syntax error naming a line that is fine.
# up.bat is the Windows entry point, so it is the one file that MUST be CRLF.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf

# Never touched: rewriting a byte here corrupts the artifact.
*.bin     binary
*.onnx    binary
*.mp4     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.docx    binary
